Chapter: ScopeHandlerClause
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The second site. A handler clause's resume binder does not
 escape the clause body -- not into the next clause, not into the handled
 expression, and not past the `with`.

 The same shape as the try regions, and the same reason it was invisible.
 The type checker was already right: `infer-handle-clauses` binds the
 resume name into a fresh env for the clause body and hands the ORIGINAL
 env to the next clause, so a later read of that word types against
 whatever was in scope all along. The name resolver was already right
 too: `resolve-handle-clauses` forks per clause. Only the emitter was
 not -- `add-local` prepends to a flat list and nothing popped it, so
 `lookup-local` found the clause's slot for the rest of the function.

 So `resume` below is a chapter-level Text. Inside the clause it is the
 Integer-taking continuation, which is what the clause means. After the
 `with`, it is the Text again -- and the emitter used to hand that
 concatenation the continuation's slot instead.

 What must NOT change: `tick` stays bound after its clause on purpose.
 The op-name is added AFTER the clause body because the handled
 expression reads the handler slot through it, and a multi-op handler
 needs every op still bound when its body runs. The restore therefore
 goes between the body and the op-name add, not at the end of the clause.
 `handler-smoke`'s multi-op and nested cases are the ones that fail if
 that ordering is got wrong.

Section: Effects

  effect Counter where
    tick : [Counter] Integer

Section: Definitions

  resume : Text
  resume = "GLOBAL"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing =
    let r1 = with Counter tick
      tick (resume) = resume 42
    in act
      print-line-uni ("counter: " & show r1)
      print-line-uni ("after: " & resume)
    end
